equately, can concentrate very well, solves problems extremely well, takes responsibility seriously, regarded as know-it-all, has a hot temper.\n\nWe need to evaluate criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, professional communication, responsibility, adaptability.\n\nLet's see strengths/concerns:\n\nA: quick correct decisions (good decision quality), calm in crisis (stress), understands tech (competence), but difficulty communicating ideas (crew cooperation, professional communication), egocentric (cooperation), not willing to further education (adaptability). So mixed.\n\nB: very conscientious (reliability), handles stress well (stress), good at assessing weather (technical), excellent computer skills (technical), but weak memory for numbers (attention), makes nasty remarks (crew cooperation, communication). So strong reliability but poor teamwork.\n\nC: anticipates dangerous situations (stress resilience, decision), sees complex connections (technical), excellent spatial vision (technical), very good leadership (crew cooperation, responsibility), but show-off (cooperation), not open to new ideas (adaptability). So strong technical and decision, but poor adaptability and teamwork.\n\nD: responds to unexpected events adequately (stress), concentrates well (attention), solves problems extremely well (technical/decision), takes responsibility seriously (responsibility), but hot temper (crew cooperation), regarded as know-it-all (cooperation). So strong technical, responsibility, but poor teamwork.\n\nWe need best overall fit, balancing strengths and concerns. Which candidate has least serious concerns? B has nasty remarks, which is serious for crew cooperation. A has egocentric and communication difficulty, also serious. C has show-off and not open to new ideas, also problematic. D has hot temper and know-it-all, also problematic.\n\nAll have some concerns. Which concerns are most severe? Nasty remarks (B) could affect safety. Hot temper (D) also. Show-off (C) maybe less severe than nasty remarks? Egocentric (A) also.\n\nWe need to weigh strengths. B is very conscientious, handles stress, good weather assessment, computer skills. But memory for numbers weak (attention). Could be mitigated? Might be serious for flight numbers. A quick decisions, calm, tech understanding, but poor communication and unwillingness to learn. That could be a problem for long-term development.\n\nC anticipates danger, sees connections, spatial vision, leadership. But not open to new ideas, show-off. Could hinder adaptability.\n\nD solves problems, concentrates, responsibility, but hot temper.\n\nWhich candidate overall best? Possibly Candidate C has strong technical and decision, leadership, but concerns about openness. However, leadership may offset some. But not open to new ideas could be risk.\n\nCandidate B's nasty remarks is a red flag for crew cooperation, which is critical. So maybe not B.\n\nCandidate A's egocentric and communication difficulty also red flag.\n\nCandidate D's hot temper also red flag.\n\nAll have red flags. Need to pick the one with strongest overall fit despite concerns. Perhaps Candidate C's strengths in anticipation, complex connections, spatial vision, leadership are highly relevant for long-distance flights. Show-off may be less severe than nasty remarks or hot temper. Not open to new ideas is a concern but could be mitigated with training.\n\nThus I might recommend Candidate C.\n\nWe need to state recommendation and give evidence: mention anticipation, complex connections, leadership, and note concerns but argue they are less critical.\n\nLet's produce public message. Also need METADATA_JSON with vote."}
